ACC-020 — Glossary

Document ID: ACC-020
Title: Glossary
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the canonical vocabulary of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Glossary provides authoritative definitions for engineering cognition concepts used throughout the Atlas specifications, ensuring consistency, interoperability, and unambiguous communication.

Where a conflict exists between another specification and this Glossary, the canonical definition defined herein SHALL take precedence unless explicitly superseded by a future revision.

2. Scope

This Glossary applies to all specifications within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework, including but not limited to:

Atlas Core Concepts (ACC)
Atlas Hivemind Specification (AHS)
Engineering Understanding Specification (EUS)
Engineering Memory Specification (EMS)
Engineering Knowledge Specification (EKS)
Engineering Evidence Specification (EES)
Engineering Reasoning Specification (ERS)
Engineering Capability Specification (ECS)
Decision Support Specification (DSS)
3. Canonical Terms
Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF)

The overarching architectural framework that defines the principles, concepts, specifications, and relationships governing engineering cognition within Atlas.

Atlas Core Concepts (ACC)

The foundational ontology that defines the canonical concepts used throughout the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

Engineering Reality

The objective engineering environment consisting of physical systems, digital systems, processes, behaviors, and conditions that exist independently of Atlas.

Engineering Observation

The acquisition of information describing Engineering Reality through observation, measurement, monitoring, inspection, simulation, documentation, or other verifiable means.

Engineering Evidence

Validated and contextually relevant information that supports, contradicts, or qualifies Engineering Understanding.

Engineering Understanding

The continuously evolving cognitive representation of Engineering Reality constructed from Engineering Evidence and interpreted within Engineering Context.

Engineering Memory

The persistent preservation of Engineering Understanding together with supporting context, evidence, reasoning, and provenance.

Engineering Knowledge

Stable, validated, and reusable Engineering Understanding preserved through repeated verification and applicable across one or more engineering contexts.

Engineering Reasoning

The explainable cognitive process of deriving engineering conclusions through analysis of Engineering Understanding, Engineering Knowledge, Engineering Evidence, and Engineering Context.

Engineering Capability

A reusable cognitive function that applies engineering cognition to accomplish a defined engineering objective.

Decision Support

The structured presentation of engineering conclusions, recommendations, alternatives, confidence, and supporting rationale to assist informed engineering decision-making.

Engineering Context

The complete set of conditions, assumptions, relationships, and environmental factors required for correct interpretation of engineering cognition.

Engineering Intent

The explicitly defined engineering objective that directs engineering cognition toward a desired engineering outcome.

Engineering Constraints

The governing limitations, requirements, and boundaries that engineering cognition must satisfy.

Engineering Confidence

The explicit expression of certainty associated with engineering cognition based upon available evidence and reasoning.

Engineering Traceability

The capability to identify and verify the relationships connecting engineering observations, evidence, understanding, reasoning, decisions, and outcomes.

Engineering Explainability

The capability to communicate the reasoning, evidence, assumptions, constraints, context, and knowledge that justify engineering conclusions.

Engineering Cognition Lifecycle

The continuous process through which engineering cognition is acquired, validated, understood, preserved, generalized, applied, evaluated, and refined over time.

